1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:09,475
An account is created, enrolled, given
authority, and one day taken away again.
Each of those is a deliberate, recorded
act.

2
00:00:09,475 --> 00:00:18,550
An administrator creates the account. The
credentials are shown exactly once and are
deliberately never written to the audit
ledger.

3
00:00:18,550 --> 00:00:27,025
And what they are given is an enrolment
credential, not a standing one. It works
to sign in once, and for nothing else.

4
00:00:27,025 --> 00:00:36,000
Because somebody else chose that password
and may still know it, the console opens
here and goes nowhere else until it is
replaced.

5
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:44,075
The length rule is enforced before
anything is sent, and changing it ends
every other session that account had open.

6
00:00:44,075 --> 00:00:54,175
Only now does the console appear — and it
shows exactly what this person holds,
which so far is a bench scientist’s work
and nothing more.

7
00:00:54,175 --> 00:01:03,450
And when somebody leaves, deactivating the
account ends their sessions and revokes
every role they held, in one recorded act.
